<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=kite">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kite</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">by 549</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A kite is a tethered craft heavier than air whose wing surfaces react against the wind to give lift. It was first made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, where kites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and bamboo, and of paper after its invention in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, served to measure distances, test the wind, lift men and signal in war; by 549 CE a kite carried a message for a rescue mission. Kites spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where the fighter kite developed, and across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>, but reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> late: Konrad Kyeser described dragon kites about 1400. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a> published an account of a kite experiment on lightning in 1752, and kites served the research of the Wright brothers toward the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeroplane">airplane</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kite.jpg" alt="Kite" width="330" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco Goya · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_cometa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>by 549</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>China</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The kite has been claimed as the invention of the 5th-century BCE Chinese philosophers Mozi and Lu Ban, who are said to have flown wooden kites called muyuan.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/sericulture">Silk<span>c. 3500 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/papermaking">Papermaking<span>179–141 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=kite">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
